<article class="article-page">
<p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/guides/">Fixes</a> / BenQ ScreenBar flickering</p>
<p class="page-kicker">Desk lighting troubleshooting</p>
<h1 class="page-title">BenQ ScreenBar flickering: identify the model and power path first.</h1>
<p class="page-lead">If your BenQ ScreenBar flickers, the fix depends on the exact model and power source. The original ScreenBar, ScreenBar Halo, and ScreenBar Pro have different current requirements and controls, so match the troubleshooting step to your label before blaming the lamp.</p>

<div class="article-summary"><strong>Check this first</strong><p>Identify the exact model, use the supplied cable/adapter or a power source that meets that model’s BenQ specification, and test whether the flicker is visible to your eyes or only on camera. If the lamp still visibly flickers on a supported power source, test the supplied controls/cable and contact BenQ rather than improvising repairs.</p></div>

<div class="article-body">
<section><p class="page-kicker">Credibility</p><h2>How this guide was made.</h2>
<p>This guide is based on BenQ’s published specifications for each ScreenBar model and common USB-power failure modes seen with monitor-attached lamps. We recommend verified power paths and manufacturer support instead of improvised electrical fixes.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Model check</p><h2>The three common models do not have identical electrical requirements.</h2>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>ScreenBar</strong><p>BenQ lists 5V DC, maximum 1A, 5W.</p></div>
<div><strong>ScreenBar Halo</strong><p>BenQ lists 5V DC, maximum 1.3A. The Halo uses a wireless controller with its own batteries.</p></div>
<div><strong>ScreenBar Pro</strong><p>BenQ lists 5V DC, maximum 1.7A / 8.5W and uses a different control design from Halo. See the <a href="/gear/desk-lighting/benq-screenbar-pro/">BenQ ScreenBar Pro</a> gear notes for what to expect.</p></div>
</div>
<p>Use the current specification page for the exact revision you own. Do not assume a monitor USB port that powers one model is adequate for another.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 01</p><h2>Test the documented power path.</h2>
<p>Use BenQ’s supplied cable and, where the product includes one, the supplied power adapter. If you have been powering the lamp from a monitor USB port, compare that port’s documented output with the ScreenBar model’s requirement. A connector that fits does not establish that the source can provide the required current.</p>
<p>Change only the power source during the test. If the flicker disappears on the supported source, the previous power path was the problem.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 02</p><h2>Decide whether the lamp flickers in person or only in recorded video.</h2>
<p>If the light looks steady to your eyes but bands or flickers in a webcam or phone recording, do not immediately treat that as an LED-driver failure. Camera shutter/exposure timing can interact with the way a light is modulated. Try the camera’s anti-flicker setting, exposure, or shutter options and compare at a different brightness level.</p>
<p>If you can see the lamp itself flicker with your eyes, continue troubleshooting the lamp and power path.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 03</p><h2>Keep model-specific controls separate.</h2>
<p>Do not follow Halo controller instructions on a ScreenBar Pro or vice versa. If a controller is unresponsive, use the exact model’s BenQ manual/support procedure. Avoid cleaning internal electrical contacts or opening the lamp unless BenQ documents that procedure for the model.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Decision</p><h2>Do you need a replacement?</h2>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>No</strong><p>If a supported power source or camera setting removes the symptom, keep the light.</p></div>
<div><strong>Maybe</strong><p>If a detachable cable or external power source is part of the model, substitute only a known-compatible part before blaming the lamp.</p></div>
<div><strong>Service / replacement</strong><p>If visible flicker persists with the supplied or documented power path and controls, use BenQ support or warranty service instead of opening the light.</p></div>
</div></section>

<PartnerOffers ids={['benqScreenbar']} placement="guide-benq-screenbar-flickering" />
<section><p class="page-kicker">Two different symptoms</p><h2>Does the light flicker, or does it only look that way on camera?</h2>
<p>If the ScreenBar looks steady in person but flickers in a webcam recording, don't order a replacement yet. The camera's exposure timing may be fighting the way the light is driven. Check whether you can actually see the flicker before deciding there's anything wrong with the lamp.</p>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>Visible flicker in person</strong><p>Identify the exact ScreenBar model and try its documented power source and supplied components. A monitor USB port may not provide the current a model requires.</p></div>
<div><strong>Flicker only in video recordings</strong><p>Try the camera's anti-flicker or exposure controls. Record the camera frame rate and whether changing brightness affects the pattern.</p></div>
<div><strong>One model works, another does not</strong><p>Compare the maker's current requirements: ScreenBar, Halo and Pro have different input/current expectations and are not identical lamps.</p></div>
<div><strong>Controller unresponsive</strong><p>Use the exact model's BenQ support instructions. A Halo wireless controller is not the same mechanism as a ScreenBar Pro control system.</p></div>
</div>
<h2>Minimal evidence to collect before contacting BenQ</h2>
<p>Note the model printed on the lamp, the power adapter's marked output, whether the supplied cable is used, which port is powering it, and whether the symptom occurs to the naked eye. When the symptom is visible only in a recorded video, include the camera model and setting used. Do not open the lamp or modify its power wiring to investigate.</p>
<p>This guide intentionally does not offer a DIY electrical repair. The useful consumer-level distinction is whether the power source is documented and whether the symptom is a camera artifact or a visible lamp fault.</p></section>

